<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="notes-week-04">
    <title>Week 4</title>

    <subsection>
        <title>Chapter 3</title>

        <p>
            <alert>This is repeated from last week's reading guide.</alert>
        </p>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Sections 3.5 through 3.7 will be covered in class and you
                        won't be quizzed on them. Differential equations can be a source of anxiety,
                        but please note that any questions involving a differential
                        equation will either be about doing an integral, or
                        using a solution to a differential equation to do
                        kinematics.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Why do differential equations show up in mechanics?
                        Well, the acceleration of a particle is
                        <m>\dfrac{d^2x}{dt^2}</m>, and often we don't know the
                        acceleration as a function of time, but we know it as a
                        function of position.
                        This gives us an equation relating the position
                        <m>x(t)</m> to some of its derivatives.
                        What should you know about differential equations?
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        What should you know about differential equations?
                        Two things: First, differential equations of the form
                        <m>\dfrac{dx}{dt}=f(x)</m> can be solved by integration,
                        and we will do an example in class.
                        Usually, as you'll see in the examples, the interesting
                        part is obtaining the differential equation by applying
                        a physical principle
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Second: the solution to the differential equation is a
                        function <m>x(t)</m>, and if we know <m>x(t)</m> we can
                        do kinematics.
                        If we need to solve a differential equation that is
                        currently out of our mathematical reach but is
                        physically important, like in section 3.7, I will give
                        you the solution.
                        The point here is that sometimes the exact form of
                        <m>x(t)</m> is important to understand a problem better,
                        but you should not put your focus on the fact that you
                        don't know how to solve the differential equation.
                        You will learn how eventually.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-chap4">
        <title>Chapter 4</title>
        <p>
            <alert>Reading Quiz 5 Covers 4.1 through 4.3</alert>
        </p>
        <p>
            <alert>Reading Quiz 6 Covers 4.5 and 4.6 </alert>
        </p>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Sections 4.1 through 4.3 are the rigorous foundation for why we were able
                        to solve problems about cars, blocks, etc. without having to care about
                        every single atom in an object. Splitting up the motion of a rigid body into
                        motion of the center of mass + rotation about the center of mass is a major
                        goal that we're slowly working towards.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Example 4.1, 4.2, and 4.5 are about the motion of the center of mass. Sometimes,
                        the motion is quite intuitive. Other times, as you'll see in the
                        problems (and maybe ex 4.5 itself), not so much. The important thing for us is that
                        it is something that can be calculated.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Section 4.4 won't be critical now, but the application of example 4.6 is very
                        cool and is worth reading. Exoplanets are a hot topic in astrophysics research,
                        meaning that there is room to do research using Newtonian mechanics! The lesson here
                        is that the problem of 2 isolated particles interacting can be reduced to just 1
                        particle once you remove the motion of the center of mass (this is a change of
                        inertial reference frame!)
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Sections 4.5 and 4.6 introduce the impulse-momentum relationship. The key idea here
                        is to always think about a <term>system</term> before rushing to solve a problem.
                        Once you have defined a system, then you can apply the impulse-momentum relationship to it.
                        If your system is isolated, then this relationship tells you that the momentum of the system
                        (or just some of its components) remains constant. A particular problem might ask you to
                        think about different systems in different parts. But you can't really do anything until you
                        are clear about the system(s).
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The other lesson of this part of the chapter is that conservation principles are sort of like banking.
                        A quantity (here: momentum) changes because external agents give (deposit) or remove (withdraw) momentum.
                        A lot of the momentum problem-solving is really just accounting: how much momentum did I start with?
                        How much did I have at the end? Were there external influences? The fact that momentum is a vector makes
                        this interpretation a little weaker (we'll see some demonstrations in class), but it can be a helpful
                        picture.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </subsection>
</section>